import platformSource from '../../../../lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/platform.rs?raw';
import desktopSource from '../../../../lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/desktop.rs?raw';
import persistenceSource from '../../../../lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/persistence.rs?raw';
import widgetSource from '../../../../lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/widgets/mod.rs?raw';
import launcherSource from '../../../../lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/launcher/mod.rs?raw';
const sections: [string, string][] = [['composition', '01 · Composition root'], ['state', '02 · State & startup'], ['launcher', '03 · Launcher flow'], ['surfaces', '04 · Bar, dock & geometry'], ['preview-assets', '05 · Prepared images'], ['widgets', '06 · Widget drag flow'], ['clients', '07 · Other native clients'], ['extend', '08 · Where to make changes']];
const state = [
  ['Durable preferences', 'DesktopSettings', 'Widget positions/visibility, pins, notes and palette mode. Loaded from the settings port; a revision-aware child coalesces edits before ordered save effects.'],
  ['Observed external state', 'apps, compositor, clock, system, media, weather, wallpapers', 'Snapshots and discovered data. Changed by worker results, not by view functions. Absence and failures follow each service’s current policy.'],
  ['Interaction state', 'launcher::Launcher; widgets::Host', 'Launcher privately owns mode/query/results/selection/scroll. The widget host privately owns drag origins/previews, calendar navigation and its timer. Typed messages change each feature’s state.'],
  ['Presentation state', 'panel motions, carousel, images, viewport', 'Time-dependent scalar transitions, decoded assets and current logical surface size. These are not domain entities.'],
  ['Composed child state', 'launcher::Launcher, widgets::Host, notifications::Center, wallpaper_selection::Selection', 'Each owns a cohesive interaction or operation. The parent maps messages and effects and coordinates shared settings and adapters.'],
];
const surfaces = [
  ['bar', 'Top', 'No keyboard capture', 'A 48-pixel surface and exclusive zone cover the top inset and capsules; Hyprland adds the 16-pixel window gap once. Only controls receive pointer input.'],
  ['widgets', 'Bottom', 'On demand', 'A full-output layer below normal windows. Widget hit regions allow clicks through the empty desktop.'],
  ['dock · closed', 'Top', 'None', 'The native surface fills the output, but its painted dock and input regions occupy the bottom portion.'],
  ['dock · open', 'Overlay', 'Exclusive', 'The same client surface hosts the launcher/pickers and captures outside clicks for dismissal.'],
  ['notifications', 'Overlay', 'On demand', 'Visible when the child has toast/history content; input remains limited to interactive regions.'],
];
---
<DocGuide chapter="client" title="Building the desktop client" heading="One framework." accent="A concrete desktop." description="Trace the production client from injected ports to launcher actions, bar layout, draggable widgets and independent native menus." sections={sections}>
  <section id="composition"><span class="eyebrow">01 / THE EXECUTABLE CHOOSES</span><h2>Adapters enter through<br />the composition root.</h2>
    <p><code>Desktop::new(DesktopAdapters)</code> receives shared trait objects. The only desktop module that selects production service implementations is <code>platform.rs</code>. Tests can construct the same desktop with fake adapters, deterministic snapshots and bundled assets.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(platformSource, 'pub fn desktop_adapters()', 'pub struct NativeAssets;')} lang="rust" label="Live source · production dependency injection" />
    <p>The compositor is optional: without a valid connection, launching apps still works, but workspace/focus behavior is unavailable. <code>AssetPort</code> stays at the presentation boundary because it returns image data. Domain services return domain values.</p>
    <p>The root implements the framework’s <code>Component</code> and <code>Application</code> contracts. Its launcher is a child controller with private state and typed messages. <code>launcher::Input</code> borrows current app, settings and appearance snapshots; it does not copy the desktop or retain adapter access. Notifications and wallpaper selection have their own child state. Widget coordination now follows the same explicit-input pattern; the bar remains root-owned.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="state"><span class="eyebrow">02 / STATE HAS DIFFERENT LIFETIMES</span><h2>What changes,<br />and where it lives.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Lifetime</th><th>State</th><th>Owner / behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>{state.map(([kind, fields, detail]) => <tr><td>{kind}</td><td><code>{fields}</code></td><td>{detail}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>At startup, <code>init</code> queues discovery of applications, settings and wallpapers. <code>Message::Loaded</code> installs that data, ranks the initial app list, schedules asset loading and invalidates the surfaces. A failed settings load leaves saving disabled to protect the original file. Application-discovery failure currently produces an empty list rather than a dedicated error screen.</p>
    <p>Subscriptions provide compositor and notification streams, clock samples every second, system samples every five seconds, media every two seconds and weather every fifteen minutes. These are independent worker streams. Updates compare relevant fields before repainting: a clock tick redraws the minute clock only when its displayed minute/date changes; a visible running timer can update each second.</p>
    <p><code>persistence::Persistence</code> is the sole producer for the settings queue. It owns one active revision and the latest immutable preference snapshot. New edits replace pending intent; an accepted write finishes first. Completion acknowledges its revision and starts a newer pending snapshot. Failure keeps the latest edit in memory and retains its error until a retry or newer save succeeds. Other action results cannot clear this feature-owned error.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(persistenceSource, '    pub fn request(', '    fn start(')} lang="rust" label="Live source · coalesce preferences before worker admission" />
    <p>The client supplies save status to the Notes view. Retry returns a child intent; the root delegates it to persistence. The same operation is available as <code>lucent-cli settings retry</code>. The existing domain port and filesystem adapter are unchanged. A successful acknowledgement means the adapter completed its write/rename; it is not a new fsync or power-loss durability guarantee. Pending edits are not flushed automatically when the process is terminated.</p>
    <p>The view reads snapshots; it does not fetch them. The renderer consumes the resulting scene; it does not know whether a value came from Hyprland, a fake adapter or a saved setting.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="launcher"><span class="eyebrow">03 / KEYSTROKE TO APPLICATION</span><h2>Follow one launcher action.</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps"><li>Super+Space reaches the desktop command socket. <code>Message::Launcher(launcher::Message::Toggle)</code> is delegated to the child through <code>Effects::delegate</code>.</li><li>The child opens Apps, clears query/selection, ranks cached applications through the pure search use case, retargets motion, and requests a dock redraw.</li><li><code>launcher::View</code> reads private child state, borrowed snapshots and decoded <code>Artwork</code>. <code>Element::map(Message::Launcher)</code> lifts its handlers into the root message type. No view calls an OS port.</li><li>Typing emits <code>launcher::Message::Query</code>. Arrows update selection and its visible row window; Tab and Shift+Tab cycle the five modes. The child owns keyboard routing only while its dock surface is open.</li><li>Enter returns <code>Intent::Launch(AppId, false)</code>. The parent queues the existing launch effect and sends Close to the child. Palette, wallpaper and widget selections likewise return explicit intents for the parent to coordinate.</li><li>The worker invokes the launch/focus use case with injected adapters. <code>Message::Completed</code> returns success or an actionable error to the UI thread. A dock click still passes <code>prefer_running = true</code>.</li></ol>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(launcherSource, '#[derive(Clone)]\npub enum Message', '/// Borrowed feature data.')} lang="rust" label="Live source · child messages and parent-facing intents" />
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(desktopSource, '    fn update_launcher(', '    fn update_widgets(')} lang="rust" label="Live source · borrow snapshots, delegate effects, coordinate intent" />
    <p>This is explicit composition, not a second event loop. The runtime calls Desktop; Desktop calls the child on the same UI thread. The child’s update accepts current borrowed inputs and can return an intent, so it is a client controller rather than another top-level Application implementation. The existing framework mapping/delegation API is sufficient; no new framework port is needed for this ownership change.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(desktopSource, '    fn launch(&self,', '    fn perform(')} lang="rust" label="Live source · client effect around the launch use case" />
    <p>The fill and outline of a selected row come from the same controlled state on the same frame. There is no delayed selection-background animation. Panel width/height and wallpaper-carousel motion have separate timelines; changing a row does not need to animate the entire panel.</p>
    <p>App icons use exact desktop-ID mappings to Lucent’s monochrome SVG catalog, with XDG icons and a bundled symbol as fallbacks. Their loading belongs to the asset adapter; row rendering receives a cached image. Search, app launching and icon resolution remain separate responsibilities.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="surfaces"><span class="eyebrow">04 / NATIVE SURFACES, CLIENT GEOMETRY</span><h2>The bar and launcher<br />share a layout contract.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Wayland layer</th><th>Keyboard</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody>{surfaces.map(([name, layer, keyboard, detail]) => <tr><td>{name}</td><td>{layer}</td><td>{keyboard}</td><td>{detail}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>A native surface’s size is not necessarily the painted panel’s size. The dock uses a full-output transparent surface so its panel can move and grow. Its rounded panel, children and input regions are ordinary elements inside that surface. Hyprland manages the surface’s layer and focus; the client manages its internal layout.</p>
    <p><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/shell_layout.rs')}>shell_layout.rs</a> contains pure desktop geometry shared by animation targets and views. Settled outer panels use the 16-pixel shell grid; control placement follows an 8-pixel rhythm with a 4-pixel inset subdivision. Launcher/input rows are 48 pixels. Height is derived from actual header, body, gaps and padding instead of copied totals for each mode.</p>
    <p>Whole rows determine launcher capacity. The last selected result remains reachable, reset stays in a reserved footer and theme cards use equal widths with their actual token gap. Dock slots are centered with equal side insets. Available space is rounded down before placement; leftover display pixels can make a grid-aligned center slightly different from the mathematical center.</p>
    <p><code>BarLayout</code> derives capsule widths from control slots and gaps. Optional media and clock capsules appear only when their calculated bounds fit between the other groups. A compact workspace strip retains the active workspace. Text is constrained inside its slot; a long track name cannot push another capsule off screen.</p>
    <p>These are client layout decisions. The framework supplies rows, constraints and clipping; the renderer supplies rounded shapes. Neither needs a special case for Lucent’s launcher or bar. During morphs, dimensions remain continuous between settled grid targets.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="preview-assets"><span class="eyebrow">PRESENTATION ASSET OWNERSHIP</span><h2>Pixels have an owner<br />before they reach the GPU.</h2>
    <p>The desktop keeps symbols/application icons separate from local wallpaper previews. <code>previews::Previews</code> owns the latter and receives an injected <code>AssetPort</code> adapter. The root supplies the current and target carousel ranges; painting uses the same range helper. The launcher receives a read-only view of prepared pixels and failures, without scheduling or adapter access.</p>
    <p>The coordinator submits one thumbnail task at a time. Rapid navigation replaces demand while the accepted decode finishes; obsolete results are dropped. A prepared preview from a committed wallpaper takes precedence over an older speculative decode. Failures stay visible until an explicit retry or a new visit, avoiding an automatic failure loop. NativeAssets performs decoding and mip preparation on the worker.</p>
    <p>Demand and cache entries are capped at 16, with previews limited to 1024 pixels per side. Unwanted entries are evicted against a 64 MiB reuse budget; wanted images stay pinned and can exceed that byte budget within the entry/dimension limits. Closing the local browser reduces reuse to 8 MiB after its transition settles. This is a client pixel-storage policy, separate from the renderer's GPU cache and process RSS.</p>
    <p><code>lucent-cli inspect</code> reports prepared icon, local-preview, online-preview and background storage. The total counts shared immutable allocations once. It excludes in-flight messages/work, decoder scratch, scene references, fonts and driver allocations. Online results still retain a provider page; their lifetime and discovery errors remain separate audit work.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="widgets"><span class="eyebrow">06 / POINTER TO PERSISTED POSITION</span><h2>Dragging is an interaction.<br />Snapping is a use case.</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps"><li>The framework recognizes a drag and emits deltas relative to its start.</li><li><code>Message::Widgets(widgets::Message::Move)</code> routes to the child, which records the widget’s initial placement once in its private drag state. It starts from the visible, clamped position and adds each delta to that origin.</li><li><code>constrain_placement</code> returns a bounded preview without mutating preferences. The host retains it and invalidates the widgets surface; the view prefers this preview over the saved placement.</li><li>While an origin is active, the view adds decorative grid elements using the same spacing token as snapping. They have no input handlers.</li><li>On release, <code>snap_placement</code> rounds the final position, then <code>move_widget</code> clamps and commits it to the shared settings. The preview is removed, hiding the grid, and a typed <code>Intent::Save</code> asks the root to persist the result through its settings adapter.</li></ol>
    <p>Dragging remains smooth between grid points; snapping happens on release. The grid is generated in the client’s widget view, so the renderer receives the same generic elements as any other decoration.</p>
    <p><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/widgets/catalog.rs')}>The widget catalog</a> contains calendar, clock, weather, media, system, notes and focus timer definitions. A single entry supplies selector text, token-derived size/default position and a view function. The same catalog validates widget IPC. The framework registry is a small table built for the current borrowed view context; it owns no snapshots or adapters. This remains compile-time composition.</p>
    <p><code>widgets::Host</code> owns transient drag state, month navigation and the focus timer. <code>widgets::View</code> borrows settings, clock/system/media/weather snapshots and prepared artwork. Its elements map child messages through <code>Element::map(Message::Widgets)</code>. Updates borrow the root's settings aggregate for the duration of the call; no second preferences copy is retained. Save, retry and media-action intents return to the root, which coordinates persistence or queues the relevant adapter call. The widget only reads save status; it does not invoke SettingsPort.</p>
    <p>Saving another preference during a drag sees only the committed layout. Hiding a widget, resetting positions or invalid geometry cancels the rest of that gesture, including its eventual release. A later new drag can start normally; unknown IDs cannot create drag state. Invalid geometry returns an error without saving. These rules belong to the client interaction controller; finite positions, snapping and bounds remain pure domain/use-case behavior.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(widgetSource, '#[derive(Clone)]', '#[derive(Default)]')} lang="rust" label="Live source · widget messages, intents and borrowed update input" />
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(desktopSource, '    fn update_widgets(', '    fn launch(&self,')} lang="rust" label="Live source · child update followed by root-owned persistence or action" />
  </section>
  <section id="clients"><span class="eyebrow">07 / REUSE BEYOND THE DESKTOP</span><h2>Other clients use<br />the same engine.</h2>
    <p><strong>Notifications:</strong> the parent maps the child’s elements with <code>Element::map(Message::Notifications)</code> and routes updates through <code>Effects::delegate</code>. The child owns toast/history presentation, paging, DND and actions. The D-Bus adapter and pure inbox policy remain outside it. History is currently in memory.</p>
    <p><strong>Keybindings and menus:</strong> <code>lucent-menu</code> is an independent client with pure selection policy. It returns the original selected value after destroying its keyboard surface. Omarchy’s per-user adapter supplies installed binding/menu data and dispatches actions. Back/Escape and nested menus use the native picker; trusted user actions retain precedence. See <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-menu.md')}>the menu integration contract</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Lock and login:</strong> both use a shared <code>SessionScreen</code>, injected authentication and native rendering. Lock uses the secure session-lock runtime; login uses greetd. A decorative layer-shell window cannot replace that protocol. Secrets stay outside scene and texture data.</p>
    <p><strong>Still supplied by the system:</strong> Hyprland handles windows, tiling, keybindings and decorations. Headless system services provide idle, clipboard and authorization backends; Lucent owns their shell presentation. The native clients do not yet replace every stock shell feature; see the <a href={withBase('/docs/system/')}>ownership guide</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="extend"><span class="eyebrow">08 / A PRACTICAL SOURCE MAP</span><h2>Change the owning layer.</h2>
    <ul class="resource-list">
      <li><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/desktop.rs')}>desktop.rs ↗</a><span>Typed messages, state transitions, effects, subscriptions and surface declarations.</span></li>
      <li><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/views.rs')}>views.rs + shell_layout.rs ↗</a><span>Launcher/bar/dock presentation and shared pure geometry. Add a geometry assertion when changing capacity or bounds.</span></li>
      <li><a href={sourceUrl('lucent/apps/lucent-desktop/src/widgets/catalog.rs')}>widgets/catalog.rs ↗</a><span>Add one definition with a stable ID, view and geometry. Keep interaction in widgets/mod.rs and presentation in widgets/view.rs.</span></li>
      <li><a href={sourceUrl('design/tokens.json')}>design/tokens.json ↗</a><span>Primary scales, semantic roles and component recipes. Regenerate the Rust and website consumers.</span></li>
      <li><a href={withBase('/docs/domain/#ports')}>Domain ports and adapters →</a><span>Add or replace an external capability here, then select it in platform.rs.</span></li>
      <li><a href={withBase('/docs/#quality')}>Validation workflow →</a><span>Pure decisions, scene geometry, reviewed Vulkan baselines and real input checks cover different failure modes.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Existing examples and clients demonstrate framework reuse. Remaining architectural work includes separating bar/dock coordination, replacing selected polling adapters and making loading/error presentation consistent. Those changes can preserve the port contracts and rendering engine.</p>
  </section>
</DocGuide>
